MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The engine knew funding_collected and nothing else. NO FEE WAS EVER BOOKED.
No balance was ever reconciled against the venue's wallet. There was no
statement and no journal, so there was no way to answer the only question a
client actually asks: what did I earn, and where is it.

INVENTORY D11 has said so since 0033 — "cumExecFee carried per leg; booking
it is Phase E" — and 0043 showed what the gap costs: funding was booked on
the entry price for four years, a quarter of it missing, and nothing in the
system could notice because nothing added the books up.

THE ONE RULE. Every entry balances to zero in USD or it is REFUSED —
nothing written, an exception rather than a warning. That single rule makes
a P&L checkable instead of asserted: a fee that vanishes, a funding payment
booked twice, a realised gain with no matching cash each fail to balance,
and each fails at the moment it happens rather than in a statement.

APPEND ONLY. No delete and no edit; a test asserts the methods do not
exist. A mistake is corrected by a reversing entry that is itself in the
journal. A ledger you can edit cannot detect a bug, a partial fill or a fat
finger — reconcile_pair's argument since 0022, applied to money.

WHY IT WRAPS THE BROKER. A ledger the engine must remember to call is a
ledger the engine can forget to call, and the forgetting is silent. The
broker is the engine's ONLY path to the venue, so LedgerBroker decorates it
and a fill is booked because there is nowhere else for it to go. Delegation
is explicit — no __getattr__ forwarding whatever it is handed — because the
question for each new broker method is always "does this move money?", and a
wrapper that guesses answers it silently and wrongly.

THE CROSS-CHECK. The replay keeps running totals; the journal builds the
same number out of 314 postings that each had to balance first.

              replay   ledger (realised)  + open position   agrees
  no impact   31,498.25     31,489.84            8.41        yes
  impact      29,766.40     29,768.92           -2.52        yes
  borrow 5%   15,040.78     15,032.18            8.60        yes

To the cent, and fees agree exactly. The open position's mark is reported
separately and never blended in: an open position's value is an estimate and
this file holds what is not estimated.

DID THE HEDGE HEDGE?

  perp realised  $-145,787   inventory  $+144,836   residual  $-951

Four years, BTC from $23k to $110k, and the legs cancelled 99.35% of it.
That residual IS the basis — the only price exposure a delta-neutral overlay
runs. Not expected to be zero; expected to be small, and now measured out of
a journal rather than asserted in a pitch.

F3 goes from open to partial: the spot BUY fee is booked in BTC where the
venue takes it, and hedgeable_btc() returns what the WALLET holds rather
than what filled. An unknown fee is never booked as zero — they are counted
and the statement says how many the venue never stated.

LIVE, not offline: build_bot wraps the real CarryBroker, the journal loads
from the new carry_ledger table at startup and tick() posts funding and
writes it back. Funding does not pass through the broker so it is posted
from the number the ENGINE reports rather than recomputed; 0043 is the
record of what recomputing it costs. Journal.from_rows re-validates every
entry on load, so a database edited by hand fails at STARTUP rather than in
a statement, and a test asserts build_bot raises on a tampered journal.

test_dockerfile caught that main.py now imports ledger and the image did not
ship it — the closure check doing exactly its job.

Still open: nothing has reconciled against a real wallet, fees_unknown is 0
only because the harness always states a fee, and no open position is
revalued into the journal by design.

